#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -e

# 1. Update system & install dependencies
sudo apt-get update -y
sudo apt-get install -y nginx ufw

# 2. Provision Directory & Permissions
sudo mkdir -p /var/www/static-site/public
sudo chown -R $USER:$USER /var/www/static-site

# 3. Configure Firewall
sudo ufw allow 22/tcp
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
echo "y" | sudo ufw enable

# 4. Deploy Nginx Configuration
sudo rm -f /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/default
sudo ln -sf /etc/nginx/sites-available/static-site /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/

# 5. Syntax Audit & Service Reload
sudo nginx -t
sudo systemctl reload nginx



**************************************************

Code Explanation
sudo ufw allow 22/tcp: Opens port 22 for TCP traffic, which is the standard port for SSH (Secure Shell). 
This ensures you do not lose your remote connection to the server.
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp: Opens port 80 for TCP traffic, which is the standard port for unencrypted HTTP web traffic.
This allows users to access websites hosted on the server.
echo "y" | sudo ufw enable: Automatically turns on the firewall.
The echo "y" | part pipes a "yes" response into the command, bypassing the interactive prompt that warns you about 
potentially disrupting existing SSH connections.

Why You Need This   [ Internet ]
        │
   (Port 22/80) ──> [ UFW Firewall ] ──> [ Server Services ]
   (Other Ports) ─X─> [ Blocked ]
Default Deny Policy: Once enabled, UFW blocks all incoming connections by default.Prevents Lockouts: Without the port 22 rule, enabling the firewall would instantly disconnect your remote session and lock you out of the server.Reduces Attack Surface: It shuts down access to thousands of unused ports, blocking hackers from scanning or exploiting background software vulnerabilities.Enables Web Traffic: It deliberately carves out a pathway (port 80) so legitimate web visitors can reach your hosted applications.


